# Shared path-based gateway for every project on this box, fronting them under one
# domain/port instead of five separate ports — the intended end state is
# https://aspireztech.com/ (this app at root) + /media, /land, /jan-score, /identity for
# the others. Every upstream is reached via `host.docker.internal` (see this gateway's
# docker-compose.yml `extra_hosts`), i.e. the SAME host-published port each project's own
# per-project Caddy already exposes — this gateway adds a routing layer in front of them,
# it does not replace or bypass any project's existing deploy.
#
# IMPORTANT — basePath: a path-mounted app (e.g. /media/*) must itself know it's mounted
# under that prefix, or its HTML will load but request its JS/CSS bundles from the wrong
# (root) path and 404. /land is built that way today (land-registry-chain/frontend's
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_PATH build arg — see its Dockerfile and next.config.js) via a
# gateway-specific image on port 3082, alongside its normal, unprefixed standalone deploy
# on 8082. /media, /jan-score, and /identity have the equivalent basePath support already
# written into their frontends (media-ops/jan-score: NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_PATH;
# p-iden: VITE_BASE_PATH) but have NOT yet had a gateway-flavored image built and deployed
# — until that happens, those three routes below will serve broken (unstyled,
# non-functional) pages, not because the routing is wrong, but because those upstreams are
# still serving root-relative assets. This is a known, temporary, documented gap — see
# deploy-infra/gateway/README.md for what "finishing" each one involves.

:80 {
	handle /land* {
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:3082
	}

	handle /media* {
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:8083
	}

	handle /jan-score* {
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:8081
	}

	handle /identity* {
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:8084
	}

	# Aspirez is the flagship/root site — no basePath needed, it already serves at "/".
	handle {
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:8080
	}
}
